    {
      "kind": "statement",
      "eyebrow": "01 · The thesis",
      "title": "The technology is not the story. The cost line is.",
      "body": "Industrials sell uptime, throughput and safety. Labour scarcity is the forcing function: automation, autonomy and digital twins improve output per worker without requiring new demand. The winners own the installed base and the service relationship; the losers sell interchangeable metal."
    },
    {
      "kind": "points",
      "eyebrow": "02 · Sector economics",
      "title": "What this sector actually sells",
      "body": "Revenue is project- and backlog-driven, with aftermarket service carrying much higher margins than original equipment. Working capital and execution risk decide cash conversion.",
      "bullets": [
        "Original equipment is cyclical and competitive; aftermarket parts and service earn the margin.",
        "Backlog visibility matters, but book-to-bill and cancellation rates matter more.",
        "Labour is a large share of both manufacturing and field service cost.",
        "Project businesses carry execution and warranty risk.",
        "Electrical equipment demand is increasingly set by grid and data-centre capex."
      ]
    },
    {
